    "blurb": "The show delivers a compact, high‑energy half hour featuring Cold Rain And Snow, a scorching Hard To Handle, and a seamless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition. Jerry’s guitar soars while Phil anchors the groove; the post‑Midnight Hour jam adds a Santana‑flavored pulse.",
    "review": "On February 4 1970 at the Family Dog on The Great Highway, the Dead offered a concise yet powerful set that captures the band in a focused early‑’70 mode. Opening with Cold Rain And Snow and moving into a fiery Hard To Handle, Garcia’s solos were described as “gliding over tribal funk,” while his vocal on Black Peter ranks among his best. The centerpiece of the night is the flawless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue that rolls straight into a tight Saint Stephen > Not Fade Away > Saint Stephen medley, demonstrating the group’s knack for seamless transitions. Phil Lesh provides a solid, Santana‑style bass foundation during the post‑Midnight Hour jam, adding extra groove to an otherwise restrained set. Although one source suffers from hiss and distortion, the clearer Cotsman soundboard captures the energy and tightness of the performance, making this show a worthwhile snapshot of the Dead’s evolving live chemistry.",
